  "worked_solution": "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(4)+{f}^{\u2032}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(4)}{2!}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(4)}{3!}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{\u2032}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(4)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(4)}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(4) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(4) & =-2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(4) & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =1-1(x-4)+\\frac{(-2)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{12}{6}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =1-(x-4)-(x-4)^{2}+2(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
